# Template for $DEPLOY_DIR/.env.prod on the production box. The real file never
# enters git. docs/how-to/deploy-to-the-vps.md has the bootstrap.
#
# Two kinds of setting live here. The backend's own, which reach the containers
# through `env_file`, and the compose-level ones further down, which the deploy
# reads through `docker compose --env-file .env.prod`. Every compose-level one
# defaults to what the original VPS runs with, so leave a line out to keep it.

# --- The backend ---------------------------------------------------------------

# The password comes from the bootstrap. The host is the shared `postgres`
# container on the web network, reached by name. Nothing in the backend opens
# this connection yet; only the migrate container reads it.
KASTEN_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://kasten_prod:REPLACE_ME@postgres:5432/kasten_prod

# Where the vault is mounted inside the container. The host side of that mount
# is KASTEN_DATA_DIR below, never this.
KASTEN_VAULT_PATH=/vault

# Where an imported Anki deck is written. The default is `03 Flashcards`.
KASTEN_FLASHCARDS_PATH=00 Inbox

# Where the agent tokens are kept. The directory above it is what
# deploy/compose.yaml mounts, never this file: `os.replace` over a bind-mounted
# file fails with EBUSY and every mint would break.
KASTEN_TOKENS_PATH=/agent-data/tokens.json

# The Host the MCP endpoint answers to: the bare name Caddy serves, with no
# scheme and no slash. Left empty, the SDK's rebinding guard answers 421 to
# everything arriving through Caddy.
KASTEN_AGENT_HOST=kasten.example.com

# --- Compose ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the images come from. A fork publishes to ghcr.io/<its owner>.
#KASTEN_IMAGE_REPO=ghcr.io/pgoell

# The uid and gid the backend and shell run as. They write the vault through a
# bind mount, so make them the owner of the data directory.
#KASTEN_UID=1000
#KASTEN_GID=1000

# The external Docker network Caddy and these containers share.
#KASTEN_NETWORK=web

# Who a commit made in the shell is by, seeded into its jj and git config on
# start. Set here, they also reach the backend through `env_file`, where jj
# reads them ahead of the vault's own config, so its saves carry the same name.
# An empty value there is still a value, and the backend's saves would carry
# an empty name: comment the lines out rather than leave them blank.
#JJ_USER=Your Name
#JJ_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Not here: KASTEN_DATA_DIR, the host directory holding `vault/` and `agent/`.
# The deploy job sets it from the KASTEN_DATA_DIR repository variable, falling
# back to /home/pascal/kasten-data, and that wins over anything in this file.
# DEPLOY_DIR, where this file lives, is a repository variable the same way.

# Nothing in this file is a secret but the database password, and
# `kasten-migrate-prod` reads the same env_file.
